% Latin Modern is loaded as Type 1 through lmodern, NOT as OpenType through
% fontspec. This is deliberate and load-bearing: do not "modernise" it back.
%
% fontspec + .otf under XeTeX embeds the text as CID-keyed CIDFontType0C with
% Identity-H encoding. Some readers can reconstruct that; many cannot. Measured
% on this very document, a text extractor recovered 7 alphabetic characters from
% the two-page PDF — the name included — against 5067 from the Type 1 build.
% Applicant tracking systems and Google Docs sit on the wrong side of that line,
% so the résumé looked blank to exactly the software that reads it first, and
% failed silently. \XeTeXgenerateactualtext did not rescue it.
%
% Type 1 copies cleanly everywhere, so the text is selectable and machine
% readable. internal/render.TestDocumentUsesExtractableFonts locks this in.
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage{lmodern}
\usepackage{multicol}

